# darkarchon — tmux-based claude code worker coordination.
#
# Lightweight infrastructure for running multiple claude code instances
# in parallel tmux windows, with file-based message passing.
#
# Sourced by every script via lib/_lib.sh.

# Tool brand prefix — parent of state dirs (~/.<TOOL_PREFIX>/<team>/).
# Also names the IO dir (/tmp/<TOOL_PREFIX>-<uid>), whose path appears twice in
# the 199-char dispatch trigger — so keep it to ~22 characters or shorter, or
# dispatches fail up front with "trigger length exceeded".
TOOL_PREFIX=darkarchon

# Team name — distinguishes parallel sets of workers (e.g. multiple
# worktrees of the same repo running side by side, or per-project teams
# on the same machine).
#
# Override per-shell with DARKARCHON_TEAM. Recommended setup with direnv:
#   echo 'export DARKARCHON_TEAM=feature-x' > .envrc
#
# Workers spawned in shells with different DARKARCHON_TEAM values land in
# different tmux sessions and write to different STATE_DIR — fully isolated.
SESSION_NAME="${DARKARCHON_TEAM:-default}"

# Dispatch completion is signalled by the worker writing its result file, not by
# a wall-clock deadline. TASK_MAX_SECONDS is only a backstop hard cap for a
# wedged worker — long-running tasks run to completion under it (the old flat
# TASK_TIMEOUT=300 killed them). A worker that ends its turn without a result is
# nudged once, then failed fast, so this cap rarely matters in practice.
TASK_MAX_SECONDS="${TASK_MAX_SECONDS:-3600}"
# Confirmed-idle polls before declaring a turn ended (debounces scrape mis-reads).
TASK_IDLE_CONFIRM="${TASK_IDLE_CONFIRM:-3}"
# Idle-from-the-start polls before assuming the trigger never took (then nudge).
TASK_NOSTART_CONFIRM="${TASK_NOSTART_CONFIRM:-10}"
# Deprecated: superseded by TASK_MAX_SECONDS. Left for reference; no longer read.
TASK_TIMEOUT="${TASK_TIMEOUT:-300}"
POLL_INTERVAL="${POLL_INTERVAL:-3}"
CLAUDE_FLAGS="${CLAUDE_FLAGS:---permission-mode auto}"

# Reject a result whose first line isn't the dispatch's DONE marker. Off by
# default: attempt-scoped result paths already keep a straggler out of a retry's
# file, so a missing marker only warns (HEADER_MISMATCH on stderr). Turn on once
# your workers are seen to comply.
STRICT_RESULT_HEADER="${STRICT_RESULT_HEADER:-0}"

# Consecutive failed dispatches to one worker before dispatch-safe refuses with
# exit 15. One success resets it; --force overrides. 0 disables the breaker.
CIRCUIT_THRESHOLD="${CIRCUIT_THRESHOLD:-3}"

# How long `dispatch-safe.sh --after <ids>` waits for its dependencies before
# giving up with exit 17.
DEPS_WAIT_SEC="${DEPS_WAIT_SEC:-3600}"

# How long a message may sit undrained before `mailbox.sh outstanding` reports
# it as probably-never-delivered.
MAILBOX_OUTSTANDING_SEC="${MAILBOX_OUTSTANDING_SEC:-300}"

# Default wait for `ask.sh --blocking` / the ask MCP tool's blocking mode.
#
# Deliberately short. A blocking ask holds a tool call open, and Claude Code
# moves a call that outlives its foreground window into a background MCP task —
# at which point the worker's turn ends WITHOUT the answer, which is the one
# outcome blocking was meant to prevent. Keep this inside that window; if a
# decision genuinely needs a human minutes away, file the question
# non-blocking and let the answer arrive by mailbox.
ASK_TIMEOUT_SEC="${ASK_TIMEOUT_SEC:-90}"

# Codex worker launch flags (used by lib/start-worker-codex.sh). The bypass flag
# skips approval prompts and the sandbox so codex can work autonomously in a
# trusted local pane — mirrors how oh-my-claudecode launches its codex team
# workers. CODEX_MODEL is optional (empty = codex default model).
CODEX_FLAGS="--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox"
CODEX_MODEL=""

# Team aging thresholds, in days since a team's last activity (newest of: a
# dispatch, a worker heartbeat, a registry change, a mailbox write). The
# dashboard grades teams by these and `lib/teams.sh` uses them to pick archive
# candidates. Nothing is ever deleted on their account — they only classify.
TEAM_DORMANT_DAYS="${TEAM_DORMANT_DAYS:-7}"
TEAM_STALE_DAYS="${TEAM_STALE_DAYS:-30}"

# Host-level state root — parent of every team's state dir. Home to the things
# that are per-machine rather than per-team, chiefly agent.config (the agent
# scans every tmux pane on the box and reports under one HOST_ID).
HOST_STATE_DIR="$HOME/.${TOOL_PREFIX}"

# State directory — task history, mailbox, questions, worker registry.
STATE_DIR="$HOST_STATE_DIR/${SESSION_NAME}"

# Optional: project-specific worker prompt context, layered on top of the
# generic role prompts in prompts/. Export DARKARCHON_PROMPT_DIR to enable.
TEAM_CONTEXT_DIR="${DARKARCHON_PROMPT_DIR:-}"

# Empty workers list — workers are spawned on demand via spawn-worker.sh.
# `start.sh` will iterate this list and call spawn-worker.sh for each entry,
# so populate it only if you want a fixed roster on `start.sh`.
WORKERS=()
